<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="UDP-β-L-rhamnose biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-3261" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: L-rhamnose| is a component of the plant cell wall pectic polysaccharides |FRAME: Rhamnogalacturonans-I &quot;rhamnogalacturonan type I&quot;| and |FRAME: CPD-14581 &quot;rhamnogalacturonan type II&quot;| and is also present in diverse secondary metabolites including anthocyanins, flavonoids and triterpenoids. Rhamnosyl transferases have been shown to utilize |FRAME: UDP-L-RHAMNOSE| as the donor substrate |CITS: [1939145 ] [15020741]|, although in mung bean (|FRAME: TAX-157791|), both |FRAME: DTDP-RHAMNOSE| and |FRAME: UDP-L-RHAMNOSE| were reported to act as sugar donors for the rhamnosylation of flavonoids |CITS: [13864832 ]| (see |FRAME: DTDPRHAMSYN-PWY|). In general UDP-D-glucose is used in plants rather than dTDP-D-glucose as a precursor in the &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; synthesis of L-rhamnose. L-Rhamnose residues have also been reported to be present in fungal glycoproteins, exopolysaccharides, and the cell wall, although their physiological role remains to be fully elucidated |CITS: [22102281][19496815][12423948]|. Genes encoding a UDP-glucose 4,6-dehydratase, and a bifunctional UDP-L-rhamnose synthase with 3,5-epimerase/-4-reductase activities have been identified in the fungal plant pathogens |FRAME: TAX-318829| (referred to in |CITS: [22102281]| as &quot;&lt;i&gt;Magnaporthe grisea (Magnaporthe oryzae)&lt;/i&gt;&quot;) and |FRAME: TAX-40559| |CITS: [22102281]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The |FRAME: TAX-3702| genome contains three similar genes that were named |FRAME: AT1G78570|, |FRAME: AT1G53500| and |FRAME: AT3G14790|. All three genes encode trifunctional proteins that catalyze the three reactions that are required to convert |FRAME: CPD-12575| to |FRAME: UDP-L-RHAMNOSE|: UDP-D-glucose 4,6-dehydratase; UDP-4-dehydro-6-deoxy-D-glucose 3,5-epimerase; and UDP-4-dehydro-L-rhamnose 4-reductase. Functional domain analysis showed that the N-terminal region of these proteins is responsible for the first activity, while the C-terminal region carries out the two following activities. Mutations in |FRAME: AT1G53500| resulted in mucilage defects in seeds, due to defficiency of rhamnogalactan. All three genes were expressed in protease-deficient |FRAME: TAX-4932| yeast cells, and assays performed with the cytoplasmic fraction of the recombinant yeast cells have shown that all three enzymes are trifunctional and catalyze all three functions |CITS: [17190829]|.</Description>
